--- a/docs/4.examples/handle-session.md
+++ b/docs/4.examples/handle-session.md
@@ -148,6 +148,22 @@ Every option is optional except `password`. The `name` option is worth calling o
 > [!NOTE]
 > The `secure: true` option tells the browser to only store and send the cookie over HTTPS. When developing locally over plain HTTP, compliant browsers (notably Safari and iOS, and Chrome on some local domains) silently drop the cookie, so the session will not persist. Set `cookie: { secure: false }` during local development to work around this.
 
+## Rotate the password
+
+To change `password` without signing everyone out, set the new password and list the previous one in `extraUnsealPasswords`:
+
+```ts
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW!,
+  extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD!],
+});
+```
+
+Sessions are always sealed with `password`. A cookie sealed with an extra password still opens, and H3 reseals it with `password` on the same request, so active sessions migrate on their own.
+
+> [!WARNING]
+> Keep `extraUnsealPasswords` short: a cookie that matches no password costs one key derivation per entry, which makes invalid cookies more expensive to reject. Remove the old password once its cookies have migrated, because while it is listed anyone holding it can still forge sessions. Sessions sent in the session header are not resealed, so they only migrate when the client receives a new seal.
+
 ## Expiration
 
 Sessions have two independent expiration controls, and you can use either or both:
